1. Introduction
When data is sent from one place to another, some bits can change because of noise or a fault in the line. The receiver needs a way to know that the data it got is not the same as the data that was sent. A very simple way to do this is to add a parity bit to the data.
In this project we design a parity error detector for 8-bit data in Verilog. The transmitter makes an even parity bit from the data. The transmission channel is modeled by an error injector, which can flip one bit of the data when we want. The receiver checks the received data together with the parity bit and gives an error flag.
The main goals of the project are:
•	Design the parity generator, the error injector and the parity checker as separate modules.
•	Connect the three modules in one top module.
•	Test every module and the top module with its own testbench in ModelSim.
•	Write a Python golden model and compare its results with the Verilog design.
2. Background
2.1 Even parity
A parity bit is one extra bit that is added to the data so that the number of 1s follows a rule. With even parity, the total number of 1s in the data and the parity bit together must be even. So if the data has an odd number of 1s the parity bit is 1, and if it has an even number of 1s the parity bit is 0.
For example, the data 10110010 has four 1s, so its parity bit is 0. The data 01110000 has three 1s, so its parity bit is 1.
2.2 How an error is detected
The receiver counts the 1s again, this time including the parity bit. If the count is still even, no error is found. If the count is odd, at least one bit was changed on the way. Counting the 1s in this way is the same as taking the XOR of all the bits, because the XOR of a group of bits is 1 when the number of 1s is odd. This is why the generator and the checker are both just XOR circuits.
2.3 Limits of parity
Parity can only detect an odd number of wrong bits (1, 3, 5, ...). If two bits are flipped at the same time the number of 1s stays even, so the error is not seen. Parity also does not say which bit is wrong, so it cannot correct the data. In this project we only flip a single bit, so the checker can always detect the error [4], [5].
3. System Design
3.1 System flow
Figure 1 shows how the data moves through the system. The input data goes to the parity generator (transmitter) and then through the transmission channel. At the receiver, the parity checker looks at the received data and the parity bit and gives the result: error_flag = 0 means the data is OK, and error_flag = 1 means an error was found.
In our design the transmission channel is the error injector. It only changes the data bits. The parity bit goes directly from the generator to the checker, so only the data can be damaged.
 
Figure 1. System flow of the parity error detector
3.2 High-level design
Figure 2 is the high-level block diagram. The top module has two inputs, data_in[7:0] and error_enable. Inside, data_in goes to both the parity generator and the error injector. The generator makes parity_bit, and the injector makes data_rx, which is the data after the channel. The parity checker takes data_rx and the parity bit (called parity_rx at the checker input) and produces error_flag.
 
Figure 2. High-level block diagram
3.3 Low-level design
Figure 3 shows the same design at bit level. In the parity generator, the 8 bits of data_in go into one XOR block. In the error injector, bits 0 to 4, 6 and 7 pass straight through. Only bit 5 goes through an XOR gate with error_enable, so this bit is flipped when error_enable is 1 and left alone when it is 0. In the parity checker, the 8 bits of data_rx and the parity bit are XORed together to get error_flag.
 
Figure 3. Low-level diagram
3.4 Top module interface
Table 1 lists the ports of the top module. The signal ok_flag is just the opposite of error_flag. It is added to make the result easier to read in the waveform.
Table 1. Ports of the top module
Signal	Direction	Width	Description
data_in	Input	8	Data to be sent
error_enable	Input	1	1 = flip bit 5 of the data, 0 = no error
parity_bit	Output	1	Even parity bit made from data_in
data_rx	Output	8	Data after the error injector
ok_flag	Output	1	1 = no error found
error_flag	Output	1	1 = error found

4. RTL Implementation
The design has four Verilog files: three small modules and the top module that connects them.
4.1 Parity generator
The parity generator XORs the 8 bits of data_in. The result is 1 when the data has an odd number of 1s, which is exactly the even parity bit.
module parity_generator (
    input wire [7:0] data_in,
    output reg parity_bit
);
always @(*) begin
    parity_bit = data_in[0] ^ data_in[1] ^ data_in[2] ^ data_in[3] ^
                 data_in[4] ^ data_in[5] ^ data_in[6] ^ data_in[7];
end
endmodule
4.2 Error injector
The error injector copies data_in to data_out. If error_enable is 1, bit 5 is inverted. This models a single bit error in the channel.
module error_injector (
    input wire [7:0] data_in,
    input wire error_enable,
    output reg [7:0] data_out
);
always @(*) begin
    if (error_enable == 1'b0) begin
        data_out = data_in;
    end
    else begin
        data_out = data_in;
        data_out[5] = ~data_in[5];
    end
end
endmodule
4.3 Parity checker
The parity checker XORs the 8 received bits and the received parity bit. If the result is 0 the number of 1s is even and the data is accepted. If the result is 1 the number of 1s is odd, so an error is reported.
module parity_checker (
    input wire [7:0] data_rx,
    input wire parity_rx,
    output reg error_flag
);
always @(*) begin
    error_flag = data_rx[0] ^ data_rx[1] ^ data_rx[2] ^ data_rx[3] ^
                 data_rx[4] ^ data_rx[5] ^ data_rx[6] ^ data_rx[7] ^
                 parity_rx;
end
endmodule
4.4 Top module
The top module connects the three modules as in the system flow. The parity bit from the generator goes to the checker, the output of the error injector goes to the checker as data_rx, and ok_flag is made by inverting error_flag.
module top (
    input wire [7:0] data_in,
    input wire error_enable,
    output wire parity_bit,
    output wire [7:0] data_rx,
    output wire ok_flag,
    output wire error_flag
);
 
    parity_generator U1 (
        .data_in(data_in),
        .parity_bit(parity_bit)
    );
 
    error_injector U2 (
        .data_in(data_in),
        .error_enable(error_enable),
        .data_out(data_rx)
    );
 
    parity_checker U3 (
        .data_rx(data_rx),
        .parity_rx(parity_bit),
        .error_flag(error_flag)
    );
 
    assign ok_flag = ~error_flag;
 
endmodule
5. Verification
5.1 Environment and method
The Verilog files were simulated in ModelSim Intel FPGA Starter Edition 2020.1. Each module has its own testbench (tb_parity_generator, tb_error_injector, tb_parity_checker and tb_top). In each testbench a task called check puts a value on the inputs, waits 10 ns, compares the output with the expected value and prints PASS or FAIL. The expected value is calculated inside the testbench, so the result does not depend on the design being tested.
The golden model is a Python program. It does the same steps in a different way and gives a second result to compare with.
5.2 Test data
All testbenches use the same five test cases. The values 178, 255 and 0 are used because their parity bit is easy to check by hand: 178 (10110010) has four 1s, 255 (11111111) has eight 1s and 0 has none, so the parity bit is 0 in all three cases. Each of the first two values is tested once without error and once with error. Table 2 gives the expected result.
Table 2. Test cases and expected results
