5.7 Golden model
The golden model is written in Python without using the XOR operator. It follows these steps for each test case:
•	Change the number into 8 bits.
•	Count the 1s and set parity_bit to 1 if the count is odd, otherwise 0.
•	Flip one bit of the data if error_enable is 1.
•	Count the 1s in the received data plus the parity bit. If the count is odd, error_flag is 1, otherwise 0.
•	Compare error_flag with error_enable and print PASS or FAIL.
The output of the program is shown in Figure 8 and summarized in Table 5. All five cases are PASS.
 
Figure 8. Output of the golden model
Table 5. Golden model results
No.	Data	error_enable	Parity	Received data	error_flag	Result
1	10110010	0	0	10110010	0	PASS
2	10110010	1	0	10110110	1	PASS
3	11111111	0	0	11111111	0	PASS
4	11111111	1	0	11111011	1	PASS
5	00000000	0	0	00000000	0	PASS

The parity bit and error_flag of the golden model are the same as in the Verilog simulation for every case. One small difference is the position of the flipped bit. The Python program stores the bits from left to right, so index 5 is the sixth bit from the left, while data_out[5] in Verilog is the sixth bit from the right. Because of this, the received data in Table 5 is not the same as in Table 2 for the error cases. In both models exactly one bit is flipped, so error_flag is the same and the comparison still passes.
6. Conclusion
In this project we designed a parity error detector for 8-bit data. The parity generator makes an even parity bit, the error injector flips bit 5 when we want, and the parity checker finds the error. Each module and the top module were tested in ModelSim, and the results match the expected values and the Python golden model.
The design is simple, but it has the usual limits of parity: it can only detect an odd number of wrong bits and it cannot correct them. The tests also cover only a few data values and a single bit error.
Some things that could be done next:
•	Test all 256 values of data_in, and make the injector flip other bits.
•	Test a two-bit error to show that parity cannot detect it.
•	Let the error also change the parity bit.
